{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_3",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "table_3",
      "quote": "Table 2. Summary of Findings and outcome-level evidence profile.\n\n| Outcome                                                        | Participants/studies           | Effect                                              | Certainty   | Explanation                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      |\n|----------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------|-------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Procedural pain during blind office/outpatient EMB or sampling | 944 participants; 8 RCTs       | MD -1.87 (95% CI -2.52 to -1.22) on a 0-10 scale    | Low         | Downgraded for RoB 2 some-concern judgments in 5/8 trials and serious inconsistency (I- squared = 86.7%), reflecting substantial heterogeneity and clinically different intervention mechanisms. Publication bias remained unresolved because the number of trials was small and one registry record had no posted results. The direction of that unavailable result was unknown, and small- study effects could not be assessed reliably; publication bias was therefore not judged strongly suspected enough to warrant an additional downgrade. This domain judgment remains uncertain. PRESS |",
      "reason": "Provides a summary of the current evidence base (number of participants/studies, effect estimate, and certainty) and explicitly describes important uncertainties (risk of bias, heterogeneity, unresolved publication bias)."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_34",
      "quote": "Third, several clinically relevant studies could not be included in the continuous primary estimate because they reported medians, ranges, ordinal outcomes, binary high-pain thresholds, ambiguous variance values, or estimands outside the prespecified primary comparison. This restriction may selectively emphasize trials with extractable continuous mean/SD data. These studies remain important contextual evidence, but mixing them into the main mean-difference pool would have required additional assumptions. Fourth, safety evidence was sparse and inconsistently reported. Adverse events were not defined or collected consistently, and serious events were rare or absent in small trials. The certainty of the safety evidence was therefore very low, and the available data do not permit definitive conclusions about the comparative harms of these interventions.",
      "reason": "Explains the limitations of existing studies (incompatible reporting formats, sparse safety data) and why those limitations justify conducting the review and the chosen analytic approach."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e3",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "table_4",
      "quote": "Table 3. Prior-review crosswalk and non-pooled or sensitivity evidence.\n\n| Study                   | Why it matters                                                                             | Prespecified boundary category                                             | Handling in this review                                                                 |\n|-------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Sripha 2018             | Placebo-controlled lidocaine- spray office EMB trial                                       | Median/range only                                                          | Non-pooled; median/range only.                                                          |\n...| NCT02465320             | Completed placebo-controlled COL-1077 registry record, 187 participants, no posted results | Registry-only record without posted results                                | Used for qualitative publication-bias discussion.                                       |",
      "reason": "Provides a prior-review/previous-study crosswalk listing earlier relevant trials and describes how those prior studies were handled relative to this review, thereby acknowledging existing similar evidence and the review's rationale for inclusion/exclusion."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e4",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_34",
      "quote": "Downgraded for RoB 2 some-concern judgments in five primary trials and substantial heterogeneity. Fifth, publication bias remains unresolved. Because the primary meta-analysis included fewer than 10 trials, funnel plots and Egger's regression test were not performed. ... target different pain mechanisms and should not be interpreted as interchangeable.",
      "reason": "Explicitly notes that interventions target different pain mechanisms and cautions against treating them as interchangeable, giving a brief description of differing mechanisms relevant to intervention effects."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e5",
      "status": "MISSING",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "No evidence in the provided text or tables that a logic model or diagram showing hypothesised relationships between intervention components and outcomes was presented or considered."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 90
}

